In this lesson, we'll look at two final exponent rules that tidy up everything you've already learned. First up, the zero exponent. Any non-zero base raised to the power of zero equals one. So five to the zero is one, negative three to the zero is one, even something like x plus one, all in brackets, raised to the zero is one. The only catch is the base can't be zero, because zero to the zero is undefined. Next, the negative exponent. A negative index basically flips the term upside down. Three to the negative two becomes one over three squared, which is one ninth. And x to the negative three becomes one over x cubed. You might be wondering why these even work. Here's the quick reason. Using the quotient law, anything divided by itself, like a to the n over a to the n, gives one. But it also gives a to the n minus n, which is a to the zero. Since both answers are one, a to the zero must equal one. For negatives, a divided by a to the n plus one simplifies to a to the negative n, which is the same as one over a to the n. So the rule matches. Now let's see it in action.